// ============================================================================
// 00 — SHARED LISTS for the SharePoint-phishing chapter
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Single source of truth. Advanced Hunting has no include mechanism:
// copy the block you need into a query, and when you edit a list here,
// re-paste it into every query that embeds it (each .kql header says which
// lists it uses).
//
// CONVENTION: tenant names are CANONICAL — lowercase, "-my" suffix stripped.
// The standard extraction pattern below strips "-my" for you, so allowlists
// never need both "contoso" and "contoso-my".
// ============================================================================

// --- Standard tenant-extraction patterns ------------------------------------
// From a full URL (EmailUrlInfo.Url, UrlClickEvents.Url):
let spTenantPattern = @"https?://([a-z0-9-]+?)(?:-my)?\.sharepoint\.com";
// From a bare host (EmailUrlInfo.UrlDomain):
let spTenantDomainPattern = @"^([a-z0-9-]+?)(?:-my)?\.sharepoint\.com$";
// Usage: | extend SharePointTenant = extract(spTenantPattern, 1, tolower(Url))

// --- Our own tenant ----------------------------------------------------------
// (Placeholder org throughout this repo: "contoso". Swap in your own values.)
let ownTenants = dynamic(["contoso"]);
let ownTenantString = "contoso";                         // for lookalike checks
let ownOrgNameTokens = dynamic(["contoso corporation", "contoso corp"]);
// Accepted email domains for our org (verify this list against EAC):
let ownEmailDomains = dynamic(["contoso.com", "contoso.org"]);

// ============================================================================
// TENANT CLASSIFICATION LISTS — the heart of the detection track
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Built over time by detection/1 (review queue) → detection/2 (dossier vet)
// → paste-ready entries (TrustedPasteLine in detection/1; detection/3 for
// malicious). Consumed by detection/0a (malicious list only),
// detection/0b, incident/1a, incident/1b, detection/1, detection/3. After
// editing here, re-paste into every consumer (the Embeds: header line says
// which) — 0b/incident-1b are MIRROR twins: logic/list changes go to both.
// Rules:
//   - Provenance is mandatory:  "tenant", // added YYYY-MM-DD, evidence/INC ref
//   - Trusted ≠ immune: a trusted tenant can still host ONE compromised
//     sharer — incident/1a surfaces trusted single-sharer fan-out, 1b's
//     pivoted gates cover it behaviourally, and incident/2's targetSharer
//     scopes the containment.
//   - Malicious entries ALERT (detection/0a instantly, 0b gate-bypass;
//     P0/P1 in incident/1a/1b); nothing auto-blocks.
//   - These lists outlive the 30-day AH retention window — they are memory,
//     not config. Never prune silently; retire entries to a dated comment.
// ============================================================================
let trustedTenants = dynamic([
    // Placeholder examples showing the mandatory provenance format —
    // replace with your own vetted partner tenants:
    "fabrikam",       // added 2026-07-19, clean baseline: 124 msgs / 18 senders / 27d span — vetted via dossier
    "adventureworks", // added 2026-07-19, clean baseline: 61 msgs / 9 senders / 22d span — vetted via dossier
    "wingtiptoys"     // added 2026-07-26, long-standing partner org, dossier clean
]);
let maliciousTenants = dynamic([
    // "eviltenant", // added YYYY-MM-DD, INC-nnnn, campaign summary
]);

// --- Phish-shaped subject terms (for hunting, not detection gating) ----------
let phishSubjectTerms = dynamic([
    "shared", "sent you", "document", "invoice", "view file", "review",
    "secure", "signed", "esign", "docusign", "action required", "fax",
    "contract", "payment", "remittance", "purchase order", "rfq"
]);

